## 7. Results

All numbers in this section are regenerated from the raw records by the scripts in the repository. Unless stated
otherwise, NRMSE differences are median relative differences over datasets (real data) or tasks (synthetic data),
and "$p_{\mathrm{Holm}}$" is the Holm-adjusted p-value of a two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test within its
pre-registered family. W/L/T counts are wins, losses and ties of the first-named method.

### 7.1 Real-data benchmark

{{ tref('table11_summary') }} reports mean NRMSE, mean rank and win counts for every method at the three
training-set sizes. {{ R['128'].n_datasets }}, {{ R['512'].n_datasets }} and {{ R['2048'].n_datasets }} datasets
are large enough for $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 128, 512 and 2048 respectively (each split keeps at least 200 test
rows).

{{ table('table11_summary') }}

**Overall ranking.** The Friedman test rejects equal performance of the 15 main methods at every size
($\chi^2$ = {{ num(R['128'].friedman.chi2, 1) }}, {{ num(R['512'].friedman.chi2, 1) }} and
{{ num(R['2048'].friedman.chi2, 1) }}; all {{ pval(R['2048'].friedman.p) }}). {{ fref('fig12_critical_difference') }}
shows the mean ranks with Nemenyi critical differences. At $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 128, global routing (MAPLE-E) has
the best mean rank ({{ num(R['128'].friedman.avg_ranks['MAPLE-E'], 2) }}), followed by MAPLE-F
({{ num(R['128'].friedman.avg_ranks['MAPLE-F'], 2) }}) and the stacked classical experts
({{ num(R['128'].friedman.avg_ranks['Stack(experts)'], 2) }}). At 512 and 2048 the stacked experts rank first
({{ num(R['512'].friedman.avg_ranks['Stack(experts)'], 2) }} and {{ num(R['2048'].friedman.avg_ranks['Stack(experts)'], 2) }}),
with MAPLE-E, MAPLE-F and tuned CatBoost within one critical difference. The three standalone PFNs rank level with
tuned CatBoost at $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 128 (PFN-D: {{ num(R['128'].friedman.avg_ranks['PFN-D'], 2) }}; CatBoost-T:
{{ num(R['128'].friedman.avg_ranks['CatBoost-T'], 2) }}) but fall to ranks
{{ num(R['512'].friedman.avg_ranks['PFN-D'], 2) }} and {{ num(R['2048'].friedman.avg_ranks['PFN-D'], 2) }} at the
larger sizes.

{{ figure('fig12_critical_difference', 'Critical-difference diagrams over the 15 main methods for the three training-set sizes (mean rank over datasets; NRMSE averaged over split repeats and pretraining seeds). Thick bars join methods whose mean ranks differ by less than the Nemenyi critical difference (CD, $\\alpha$ = 0.05).') }}

{%- macro sig(rec) -%}{{ 'significant' if rec.get('p_holm_family', rec.get('p', 1)) < 0.05 else 'not significant' }}{%- endmacro -%}
**Routing versus the PFN alone (RQ5).** Routing the PFN together with the classical experts reduces PFN-D's error
at every size, by a median of {{ reduction(R['128'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_PFN-D']) }} at $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 128
({{ stat(R['128'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_PFN-D']) }}), {{ reduction(R['512'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_PFN-D']) }} at 512
({{ stat(R['512'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_PFN-D']) }}) and {{ reduction(R['2048'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_PFN-D']) }} at 2048
({{ stat(R['2048'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_PFN-D']) }}). The growing margin reflects the PFN's decline with
$n_{\mathrm{train}}$ rather than an improvement of the router. The second half of H5, that MAPLE-F is no worse than
stacking, does not hold at every size. Against the stacked experts, the median difference is
{{ gain(R['128'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)']) }} in MAPLE-F's favour at 128
({{ stat(R['128'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)']) }}; {{ sig(R['128'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)']) }}), whereas
MAPLE-F has higher median error at 512 ({{ pct(-R['512'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)'].median_diff) }};
{{ stat(R['512'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)']) }}; {{ sig(R['512'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)']) }}) and at 2048
({{ pct(-R['2048'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)'].median_diff) }};
{{ stat(R['2048'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)']) }}; {{ sig(R['2048'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)']) }}). Against
stacking that includes PFN-D, MAPLE-F has higher median error at 512
({{ pct(-R['512'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(PFN-D)'].median_diff) }}; {{ stat(R['512'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(PFN-D)']) }};
{{ sig(R['512'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(PFN-D)']) }}). All of these deficits are below the 1% threshold we set for
practical relevance. Local routing did not improve on global routing: at $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 128, MAPLE-E had
{{ pct(-R['128'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_MAPLE-E'].median_diff) }} lower median error than MAPLE-F
({{ stat(R['128'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_MAPLE-E'], flip=True) }}; {{ sig(R['128'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_MAPLE-E']) }}), and the
difference was not significant at the larger sizes either.

**Does the PFN add information to the experts?** In an exploratory comparison (raw p-values), stacking with PFN-D
had lower error than stacking the experts alone at $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 128
({{ stat(R['128'].exploratory['StackPFN_vs_StackExperts']) }}), but not at 512
({{ stat(R['512'].exploratory['StackPFN_vs_StackExperts']) }}) or 2048
({{ stat(R['2048'].exploratory['StackPFN_vs_StackExperts']) }}). At the larger sizes the two stackers produced
identical errors on {{ R['512'].exploratory['StackPFN_vs_StackExperts'].ties }} and
{{ R['2048'].exploratory['StackPFN_vs_StackExperts'].ties }} datasets respectively.

**Comparison with tuned gradient boosting (RQ6, exploratory).** At $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 128, MAPLE-F reduced the
median error of tuned CatBoost by {{ reduction(R['128'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T']) }}
({{ stat(R['128'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T']) }}), of tuned XGBoost by {{ reduction(R['128'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_XGBoost-T']) }}
({{ stat(R['128'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_XGBoost-T']) }}) and of tuned LightGBM by
{{ reduction(R['128'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_LightGBM-T']) }} ({{ stat(R['128'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_LightGBM-T']) }}). At 512 the
difference to tuned CatBoost is {{ sig(R['512'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T']) }}
({{ gain(R['512'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T']) }} in MAPLE-F's favour; {{ stat(R['512'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T']) }}),
and at 2048 tuned CatBoost has the lower median error
({{ pct(-R['2048'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T'].median_diff) }};
{{ stat(R['2048'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T'], flip=True) }}; {{ sig(R['2048'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T']) }}).

