### 7.2 Cost and scaling

{{ tref('table06_efficiency_n512') }} lists fit time, prediction time and memory at $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 512, and
{{ fref('fig08_cost_accuracy') }} places each method on the cost–accuracy plane. Tuned CatBoost is the most
expensive method, at {{ num(T6['fit time (s)']['CatBoost-T'], 0) }} s mean fit time per split on one CPU thread
({{ S.n_trials }} random configurations with 3-fold cross-validation). MAPLE-F's fit time,
{{ num(T6['fit time (s)']['MAPLE-F'], 1) }} s, is dominated by the out-of-fold fits of the five experts; the PFN
itself needs {{ num(T6['fit time (s)']['PFN-D'], 2) }} s to preprocess the training split and
{{ num(T6['predict time (s)']['PFN-D'], 2) }} s to predict. The PFN, TabPFN v2 and TabICL run on a GPU while the other methods
run on one CPU thread, so these timings are not a like-for-like hardware comparison. Pretraining costs about
{{ num(T6['pretraining (GPU-h, one-off)']['PFN-D'], 1) }} GPU-hours per checkpoint, paid once.

{{ table('table06_efficiency_n512') }}

{{ figure('fig08_cost_accuracy', 'Cost–accuracy trade-off at n_train = 512: mean fit-plus-predict time per split (log scale) against mean NRMSE over datasets. Points closer than a few pixels share one label. PFN, TabPFN v2 and TabICL timings are GPU inference; all other methods ran on one CPU thread.') }}

{%- set T14 = S.tables.table14_scaling -%}
{%- set NS = T14['PFN-D NRMSE'] | list | sort -%}
{{ fref('fig15_scaling') }} follows three datasets from $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = {{ NS[0] }} to {{ NS[-1] }} (the sizes
available for all three). With the smallest training sets the PFN is the most accurate method (mean NRMSE
{{ num(T14['PFN-D NRMSE'][NS[0]]) }}, against {{ num(T14['CatBoost NRMSE'][NS[0]]) }} for CatBoost), and its
inference time stays almost flat. Its accuracy, however, stops improving after {{ NS[-2] }} training rows
({{ num(T14['PFN-D NRMSE'][NS[-2]]) }} at {{ NS[-2] }}, {{ num(T14['PFN-D NRMSE'][NS[-1]]) }} at {{ NS[-1] }}),
while every tree ensemble keeps improving (CatBoost: {{ num(T14['CatBoost NRMSE'][NS[-1]]) }} at {{ NS[-1] }}).
Above 1024 training rows the PFN sees random 1024-row contexts averaged over bags, so it cannot use all the data;
its pretraining contexts were also no larger than this. The scaling behaviour of the standalone PFN is therefore
limited by design, and it explains why its rank falls with $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ in
{{ fref('fig12_critical_difference') }}.

{{ figure('fig15_scaling', 'Scaling with training-set size on three datasets (California housing, kin8nm, physicochemical protein; three repeats each), for the sizes available for all three. (a) Fit-plus-predict time; (b) mean NRMSE. PFN-D uses GPU inference with 1024-row context bagging above 1024 training rows; the other methods use one CPU thread and default hyperparameters.') }}

### 7.3 Synthetic generalisation

{{ tref('table09_generalization') }} and {{ fref('fig11_generalization') }} report mean NRMSE on the in-distribution
(ID) suite and the seven structurally out-of-distribution (OOD) suites. Among the methods trained at our budget,
the mathematical-prior PFNs are the most accurate on the ID suite (PFN-D: {{ num(T9['PFN-D']['id']) }}; ExtraTrees: {{ num(T9['ExtraTrees']['id']) }};
CatBoost: {{ num(T9['CatBoost']['id']) }}). Averaged over the OOD suites they are level with ExtraTrees (PFN-D:
{{ num(T9['PFN-D']['OOD mean']) }}; ExtraTrees: {{ num(T9['ExtraTrees']['OOD mean']) }}). ExtraTrees is better on
nested compositions, Voronoi regions and regime mixtures, whereas the PFNs are better on held-out families,
stretched parameters, three-way interactions and heavy-tailed noise. The PFN trained on the SCM prior is far less
accurate on every suite (ID {{ num(T9['PFN-SCM']['id']) }}, OOD {{ num(T9['PFN-SCM']['OOD mean']) }}). This result is
expected to favour the mathematical prior, because all synthetic suites come from the same generator. On real data
(RQ4) the two priors are statistically indistinguishable at every size (Section 9).
{%- if SY.get('external') and SY.external.get('TabPFN-v2') %} The external foundation models were also run on every
synthetic task. Relative to PFN-D, the median of (model − PFN-D)/PFN-D NRMSE was
{{ pct(SY.external['TabPFN-v2'].id.median_diff, signed=True) }} (ID) and
{{ pct(SY.external['TabPFN-v2'].pooled_ood.median_diff, signed=True) }} (pooled OOD) for TabPFN v2, and
{{ pct(SY.external['TabICL'].id.median_diff, signed=True) }} and {{ pct(SY.external['TabICL'].pooled_ood.median_diff, signed=True) }}
for TabICL (negative = more accurate than PFN-D; mean NRMSE ID/OOD: TabPFN v2 {{ num(T9['TabPFN-v2']['id']) }} /
{{ num(T9['TabPFN-v2']['OOD mean']) }}, TabICL {{ num(T9['TabICL']['id']) }} / {{ num(T9['TabICL']['OOD mean']) }}).
{%- if SY.external['TabPFN-v2'].id.median_diff > 0 and R['512'].exploratory['D_vs_TabPFN-v2'].median_diff < 0 %} On tasks drawn from our own generator, the small PFN
pretrained on that generator is therefore more accurate than TabPFN v2, whereas on real data the order reverses.
{%- elif SY.external['TabPFN-v2'].id.median_diff < 0 %} TabPFN v2 is more accurate than PFN-D even on the
in-distribution suite, drawn from the generator PFN-D was pretrained on ({{ stat(SY.external['TabPFN-v2'].id, flip=True) }}), so the advantage of the large model is not specific to real data.{% endif %}{% endif %}

{{ table('table09_generalization') }}

{{ figure('fig11_generalization', 'Mean NRMSE per synthetic suite (200 tasks each; PFN bars averaged over three pretraining seeds). Error bars: 95% bootstrap confidence intervals over tasks. ID = fresh tasks from the training cells; the seven OOD suites differ from training in structure.') }}

**Adaptive versus uniform prior (RQ1).** The adaptive sampler did not improve on the uniform mathematical prior.
On the ID suite, PFN-D had {{ pct(SY.RQ1_nrmse.id.median_diff) }} lower median NRMSE than PFN-A
({{ stat(SY.RQ1_nrmse.id) }}); on the pooled OOD suites it had {{ pct(-SY.RQ1_nrmse.pooled_ood.median_diff) }}
higher median NRMSE ({{ stat(SY.RQ1_nrmse.pooled_ood) }}). None of the seven OOD suites shows a significant
difference after Holm correction. H1 is not supported.

### 7.4 Behaviour of the sampler and of the disagreement signal

{{ fref('fig04_adaptation') }} shows how far each adaptive sampler moved away from the uniform prior. The
loss-driven sampler (B) moved far: its final KL divergence from uniform averaged
{{ num(AD.B.kl_final_mean, 2) }} over seeds, and its final family probabilities ranged from
{{ num(AD.B.family_prob_final_min, 3) }} to {{ num(AD.B.family_prob_final_max, 3) }} (uniform:
{{ num(1 / AD.B.n_families, 3) }}). The disagreement-driven samplers (C and D) barely moved: final KL
{{ num(AD.C.kl_final_mean, 3) }} and {{ num(AD.D.kl_final_mean, 3) }}, with family probabilities between
{{ num(AD.D.family_prob_final_min, 3) }} and {{ num(AD.D.family_prob_final_max, 3) }} for D. The reason is the scale of
the scores. Across cells, the gap scores had a standard deviation of {{ num(AD.D.score_sd_final_mean, 3) }}, against
{{ num(AD.B.score_sd_final_mean, 3) }} for the loss scores. At $\tau$ = {{ S.sampler.tau }}, such small differences
produce Gibbs weights close to uniform (Proposition 5). The sharper temperature $\tau$ = 0.05 moved the sampler
further (final KL {{ num(AD['tau_0.05'].kl_final_mean, 2) }}) without changing accuracy (Section 8).
{{ fref('fig05_family_distribution') }} shows the final family distributions: B concentrated on oscillatory
families (trigonometric and damped oscillator), whereas C and D stayed near uniform.

{{ figure('fig04_adaptation', '(a) Family probabilities of PFN-D (seed 0) over the 14 adaptation rounds (cells summed by family). (b) KL divergence of the sampling distribution from uniform for B, C and D (mean over three seeds; band: range over seeds).') }}

{{ figure('fig05_family_distribution', 'Final sampling probability of each function family (summed over complexity levels; mean over three seeds) for the loss-driven sampler (PFN-B) and the disagreement-driven samplers (PFN-C, PFN-D). Dashed line: uniform.') }}

{{ fref('fig14_learning_curves') }} shows that this re-weighting changed what the model was trained on but not what it
learned. B's own training loss is higher because it over-samples hard cells. On held-out probe tasks drawn
uniformly from all cells, however, the error curves of A, B, C and D coincide throughout pretraining.

{{ figure('fig14_learning_curves', '(a) Training loss on each variant\'s own (adaptive) prior (mean over three seeds, smoothed). (b) Excess error on held-out probe tasks drawn uniformly over cells after each adaptation round (mean over seeds).') }}

**Disagreement versus loss as the adaptation signal (RQ2).** On synthetic tasks, PFN-C had lower error than PFN-B
overall ({{ gain(SY.RQ2.all) }}; {{ stat(SY.RQ2.all) }}) and on the noisier half of the tasks
({{ gain(SY.RQ2.high_noise) }}; {{ stat(SY.RQ2.high_noise) }}). These effects are statistically
{{ 'significant' if SY.RQ2.all.p_holm_family < 0.05 else 'not significant' }} but
{{ 'practically negligible (below 1%)' if negligible(SY.RQ2.all) and negligible(SY.RQ2.high_noise) else 'above the 1% threshold' }}.
On real data the direction reversed: PFN-B had lower median error than PFN-C at $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 512
({{ pct(-R['512'].rq['RQ2_real_C_vs_B'].median_diff) }}; {{ stat(R['512'].rq['RQ2_real_C_vs_B'], flip=True) }}) and
2048 ({{ pct(-R['2048'].rq['RQ2_real_C_vs_B'].median_diff) }};
{{ stat(R['2048'].rq['RQ2_real_C_vs_B'], flip=True) }}). The first of these is
{{ 'below' if negligible(R['512'].rq['RQ2_real_C_vs_B']) else 'above' }} our 1% threshold for practical
relevance{{ ' and so is the second' if negligible(R['2048'].rq['RQ2_real_C_vs_B']) else '; the second is just above it' }},
so on real data the loss-driven sampler was, if anything, slightly better.

**Does the gap track the PFN's error (RQ3)?** {{ fref('fig13_disagreement_vs_error') }} plots the label-free gap
against the PFN's excess error. On synthetic tasks the task-level Spearman correlation for PFN-D is
{{ num(SY.RQ3['PFN-D'].spearman, 2) }} (95% CI [{{ num(SY.RQ3['PFN-D'].ci[0], 2) }},
{{ num(SY.RQ3['PFN-D'].ci[1], 2) }}], n = {{ SY.RQ3['PFN-D'].n }}), far below the
{{ num(SY.RQ3['PFN-D'].spearman_loss_vs_excess, 2) }} of the label-based loss. The committee was more accurate than
the PFN on only {{ pct(SY.RQ3['PFN-D'].frac_committee_better, 0) }} of tasks. Proposition 4(b) guarantees that the
gap tracks excess error only in that regime; elsewhere the gap also measures the committee's own error. The
correlation was weakest on three-way interactions ({{ num(SY.RQ3['PFN-D'].by_suite.ood_interact3, 2) }}) and regime
mixtures ({{ num(SY.RQ3['PFN-D'].by_suite.ood_mixed, 2) }}). On real data the noise-free target is unknown, so
excess error cannot be measured directly. Proposition 4(d) gives a label-computable proxy: the PFN's test MSE minus
the committee's, which equals the difference of their excess errors and contains no noise term. With the dataset
as unit, the gap's correlation with this proxy is {{ num(SY.RQ3_real.spearman_regret, 2) }} (95% CI
[{{ num(SY.RQ3_real.ci_regret[0], 2) }}, {{ num(SY.RQ3_real.ci_regret[1], 2) }}], n = {{ SY.RQ3_real.n_datasets }}),
and the median within-dataset correlation across splits is
{{ num(SY.RQ3_real.median_within_dataset_spearman_regret, 2) }}. Its correlation with the PFN's raw test MSE is
higher ({{ num(SY.RQ3_real.spearman_datasets, 2) }}, 95% CI [{{ num(SY.RQ3_real.ci[0], 2) }},
{{ num(SY.RQ3_real.ci[1], 2) }}]), but raw MSE is confounded by each dataset's noise level, which raises both
quantities. H3 is therefore {{ S.tables.table13_rq_verdicts.verdict.RQ3 | lower }}: the gap tracks the PFN's excess
error on synthetic tasks{% if SY.RQ3_real.ci_regret[0] > 0 %}, and on real data once noise is removed{% else %},
but we find no reliable relation on real data once noise is removed{% endif %}.

{{ figure('fig13_disagreement_vs_error', 'Label-free PFN–committee gap versus the PFN\'s error. (a) Synthetic tasks, PFN-D: excess error relative to the noise-free target (log–log; dashed line: identity). (b) Real-data splits: PFN test MSE on standardised targets against the gap on the test points.') }}

## 8. Ablation and Sensitivity Analysis

{{ tref('table07_ablation') }} and {{ fref('fig09_ablation') }} report one-factor changes to PFN-D, each pretrained
with seed 0 and compared with PFN-D seed 0 on all synthetic tasks and on real data at $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 512.
These comparisons must be read against the variation between pretraining seeds. Across its three seeds, PFN-D's
mean ID NRMSE ranged over {{ num(SY.seed_spread['PFN-D'].id_range, 4) }} and its mean OOD NRMSE over
{{ num(SY.seed_spread['PFN-D'].ood_range, 4) }}.{% if SY.seed_spread['PFN-D'].id[0] == SY.seed_spread['PFN-D'].id | max %}
Seed 0 happened to be the least accurate of the three on the ID suite ({{ num(SY.seed_spread['PFN-D'].id[0], 4) }}),
so most one-factor variants appear slightly better than the reference.{% endif %}

{{ table('table07_ablation') }}

{{ figure('fig09_ablation', 'One-factor ablations relative to PFN-D (seed 0): median relative NRMSE change on synthetic tasks (left) and on real datasets at n_train = 512 (right). Positive = worse than PFN-D. Error bars: 95% bootstrap confidence intervals.') }}

{%- set AB = S.stats.ablation -%}
{%- set T7 = S.tables.table07_ablation -%}
{%- macro holm(run) -%}{{ ('$p_{\\mathrm{Holm}}$ < 0.001' if T7['p (real)'][run] < 0.001 else '$p_{\\mathrm{Holm}}$ = ' ~ ('%.3f' | format(T7['p (real)'][run]))) }}{%- endmacro -%}
Three factors produce effects well beyond the seed variation (p-values below are Holm-corrected across the rows of
{{ tref('table07_ablation') }}). **The prior family** matters most on synthetic tasks: the SCM prior (P0) increases
NRMSE by {{ pct(AB.P0.synthetic.delta_vs_ref) }}, but its effect on real data
({{ pct(AB.P0.real512.delta_vs_ref, signed=True) }}; {{ holm('P0') }}) is not significant. **Pretraining compute**
matters clearly on synthetic tasks: a quarter of the steps costs
{{ pct(AB['budget_0.25_D'].synthetic.delta_vs_ref, signed=True) }} and half the steps
{{ pct(AB['budget_0.5_D'].synthetic.delta_vs_ref, signed=True) }}; the uniform-prior runs with the same budget cuts,
also compared with PFN-D seed 0, show the same pattern ({{ pct(AB['budget_0.25_A'].synthetic.delta_vs_ref, signed=True) }}
and {{ pct(AB['budget_0.5_A'].synthetic.delta_vs_ref, signed=True) }}). On real data the budget effects are small
(between {{ pct([AB['budget_0.25_D'].real512.delta_vs_ref, AB['budget_0.5_D'].real512.delta_vs_ref, AB['budget_0.25_A'].real512.delta_vs_ref, AB['budget_0.5_A'].real512.delta_vs_ref] | min, signed=True) }}
and {{ pct([AB['budget_0.25_D'].real512.delta_vs_ref, AB['budget_0.5_D'].real512.delta_vs_ref, AB['budget_0.25_A'].real512.delta_vs_ref, AB['budget_0.5_A'].real512.delta_vs_ref] | max, signed=True) }})
and significant for {% set ns = namespace(k=0) %}{% for r in ['budget_0.25_D', 'budget_0.5_D', 'budget_0.25_A', 'budget_0.5_A'] %}{% if T7['p (real)'][r] < 0.05 %}{% set ns.k = ns.k + 1 %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}{{ ns.k }}
of the four runs. **Function complexity** matters for transfer: pretraining on complexity level 0 only raised
real-data NRMSE by {{ pct(AB.complexity_0.real512.delta_vs_ref, signed=True) }} ({{ holm('complexity_0') }}), and
stopping at level 1 by {{ pct(AB.complexity_1.real512.delta_vs_ref, signed=True) }} ({{ holm('complexity_1') }}).
Every change to the sampler itself (score definition, committee size, temperature, floor, EMA, number of probes and
update period) changes NRMSE by less than 1% on synthetic tasks and on real data, within the range spanned by
pretraining seeds.

{{ tref('table08_sensitivity') }} and {{ fref('fig10_sensitivity') }} show the same runs as one-parameter sweeps.
The curves are flat for all sampler hyperparameters and slope clearly only for the training budget and the maximum
complexity.

